<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spanish–American War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Spanish-American War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spanish–American War was fought in 1898 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, arising from the Cuban struggle for independence. Spain was quickly defeated and lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>, Guam and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> treat it as the end of Spain's overseas empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war.jpg" alt="Spanish–American War" width="320" height="372" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Barbudo Barbudo and TEP · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Collage_infobox_for_Spanish-American_War.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The nineteenth century was a period of decline for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>, which had lost most of its American colonies in the early wars of independence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> supplied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> with prestige and trade and served as a training ground for its army. The first serious bid for Cuban independence, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-years-war">Ten Years' War</a>, began in 1868 and was subdued in 1878. In early 1895 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-marti">José Martí</a> launched a new invasion and revolt. Spanish authorities suppressed it harshly, and under Governor-General Valeriano Weyler they adopted a policy of reconcentration.</p><p>American newspapers, notably Joseph Pulitzer's New York World and William Randolph Hearst's New York Journal, denounced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and historians debate how far they swayed opinion. In January 1898 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-mckinley">William McKinley</a> sent the cruiser USS Maine to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> to protect American citizens, and on 15 February 1898 it was sunk by an explosion in the harbour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Teller Amendment of 19 April 1898 promised that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> did not intend to annex <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>. On 21 April the United States began a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of Cuba, and the two countries declared war. In the Pacific, a US squadron destroyed the Spanish fleet at Manila Bay on 1 May 1898. The first US forces landed in Cuba on 10 June, fought at El Caney and San Juan Hill on 1 July, and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de Cuba on 17 July. Guam surrendered without resistance on 20 June, and US troops landed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> on 25 July. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> was signed on 13 August. Disease killed far more soldiers than combat did.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1898">Treaty of Paris</a>, signed on 10 December 1898, ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>, Guam and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, which paid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> 20 million dollars for the Philippines. Cuba was to become independent in 1902, though in practice it became a US protectorate.</p><p>The war made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> a major power with island possessions across the globe and provoked a debate over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a>. President McKinley and the supporters of expansion prevailed over opponents led by William Jennings Bryan, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-twain">Mark Twain</a> wrote The War Prayer in protest. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> the revolution against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-american-war">Philippine–American War</a>. In Spain the defeat was a shock to national life and prompted the reassessment of the Generation of '98.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Spanish–American War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Spanish–American War</b>, Spain ceded Cuba, Puerto Rico, Guam and the Philippines to the United States by the Treaty of Paris of 1898 for US$20 million, and Cuba, which had declared independence from the Spanish Empire that year, passed under an American military government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-cuba-1902-1959">Republic of Cuba (1902-1959) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Spanish-American War</b> of 1898 the United States destroyed Spanish squadrons at Manila Bay and Santiago de Cuba within months and stripped Spain of these last major overseas possessions, although small African territories remained.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1898, during the <b>Spanish-American War</b>, the cruiser USS Charleston captured the island, and Spain ceded it to the United States.</blockquote><a href="/state/guam">Guam</a></li><li><blockquote>American troops landed at Guánica in July 1898 during the <b>Spanish-American War</b>, months after Spain had granted Puerto Rico an autonomous charter.</blockquote><a href="/state/puerto-rico-united-states">Puerto Rico (United States)</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Spanish-American War</b> made Hawaii strategically valuable, and after a treaty failed in the Senate despite petitions by many Native Hawaiians, Congress annexed the islands by the Newlands Resolution in July 1898.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-hawaii">Republic of Hawaii</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Grito de Lares revolt of 1868, Spain granted autonomy in 1897, but the United States invaded in 1898 during the <b>Spanish-American War</b>, and the Treaty of Paris transferred the island to Puerto Rico (United States).</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-puerto-rico">Spanish Puerto Rico</a></li></ul></section>
